  "id": "Compound::DB00869 - Gene::766",
  "verbalization": "Dorzolamide, a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor, is shown to bind to Ca5a and Ca6, which are involved in the reversible hydration of carbon dioxide. This mechanism is further supported by the fact that both Ca5a and Ca6 participate in carbonate dehydratase activity, which is linked to the process of carbon dioxide hydration. The presence of Ca7, which is a participant in the same metabolic pathway, suggests a potential mechanistic link between Dorzolamide and Ca7 through the regulation of carbon dioxide hydration. The cumulative evidence from the three paths indicates that Dorzolamide may influence Ca7 via the carbonic anhydrase pathway, potentially affecting the metabolic processes associated with this enzyme. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of carbon dioxide hydration in cellular function and suggests that Dorzolamide could modulate Ca7 through this route.",
